Menopause (Symptoms)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Hot flashes/night sweats, Secondary amenorrhoea >1 year, Vaginal atrophy/dyspareunia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-022
Reason for Visit: Acute severe burning and tearing vaginal pain following intercourse, resulting in minimal bleeding and significant distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, sharp vaginal pain and postcoital spotting in a postmenopausal woman, leading to severe anxiety regarding potential malignancy or a significant vaginal laceration. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively identify the bleeding source, rule out a deep vault tear requiring suturing, and provide immediate local analgesia and reassurance before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: We tried to have intercourse, and it felt like razor blades; now I am bleeding and the burning will not stop.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports progressive cessation of menses resulting in a full year of secondary amenorrhoea, marking menopause. Over the past six months, she has experienced severe vasomotor symptoms including sudden, intense hot flashes particularly at night, leading to sleep disruption and secondary fatigue. Tonight's acute presentation involves severe entry dyspareunia, described as burning and tearing, followed by light vaginal spotting. She reports a history of progressive vaginal atrophy presenting with daily dryness and burning. She also notes new-onset mood swings, irritability, thinning of scalp hair, and decreased libido.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but experiencing acute, localized discomfort when sitting.
Social/Safety Concerns: High relationship distress and acute anxiety regarding the bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears uncomfortable, sitting rigidly, and is visibly distressed by the pain and bleeding.
Physical Exam: Visual inspection of the external genitalia reveals marked signs of estrogen deficiency: the introitus is narrowed, and the mucosa is exceptionally pale, thin, and friable (classic vaginal atrophy). At the posterior fourchette, there is a superficial 1cm longitudinal mucosal fissure oozing a scant amount of blood. No deep vaginal vault lacerations or active arterial bleeding. No gross exophytic lesions or masses on the cervix or vaginal walls.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; supportive partner present.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. Has been using over-the-counter water-based lubricants with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Vaginal Atrophy (Genitourinary Syndrome of Menopause) with acute superficial coital laceration - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Vaginal Vault Tear - Ruled out by direct visualization confirming the laceration is purely superficial (mucosal) and localized to the posterior fourchette, not involving deep muscle or vault structures.

Cervical/Vaginal Carcinoma - Unlikely given the clear traumatic etiology (postcoital), the specific location of the fissure, and the absence of friable tumor masses.

Vulvovaginal Candidiasis - Ruled out by the absence of thick, white discharge and the clear presence of profound mucosal thinning rather than inflammatory erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with acute postcoital bleeding and severe burning pain on a background of classic menopausal symptoms (>1 year amenorrhoea, hot flashes). Objective physical examination definitively identified a superficial mucosal laceration secondary to profound vaginal atrophy, safely ruling out a deep, surgically actionable vault tear or a massively bleeding tumor. The acute, severe burning pain and the high level of anxiety regarding the bleeding required urgent after-hours assessment for immediate local wound care, topical analgesia, and clinical reassurance, which could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period without causing significant distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied a topical lidocaine 2% gel to the posterior fourchette to provide immediate numbness and break the pain cycle. Provided a bland, zinc-based barrier cream to protect the raw mucosa from urine. Advised strict pelvic rest.
Positive Behavioral Change: To allow the tissue to heal without stinging, pour a cup of lukewarm water over the vulva while urinating; experiencing the immediate relief from burning will reinforce this hygienic, protective habit. To rebuild the tissue integrity long-term, commit to using the prescribed vaginal estrogen cream (once ordered by your GP) consistently twice a week; observing the return of normal moisture and elasticity will ensure strict adherence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within one week to prescribe topical vaginal estrogen and consider systemic HRT.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the bleeding restarts and becomes a continuous flow (soaking a pad), or if you develop a fever or foul-smelling discharge.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The bleeding is from a benign, superficial tear; however, long-term medical management is required to prevent recurrence.
Clinical Course: The local anesthetic gel will provide immediate relief. The small tear will heal completely within 3 to 5 days if kept clean and protected.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if the bleeding becomes heavy or if you experience a sudden onset of pelvic pain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your body is no longer producing estrogen, the tissues of your vagina have become incredibly thin, dry, and fragile. The friction of intercourse caused a small "paper cut" at the entrance, which is why it is bleeding and burning so severely. The numbing gel stops the immediate pain, but you need a prescription estrogen cream from your regular doctor to thicken and heal the tissue properly.
Actionable Steps: Do not use soap on the area. Apply the barrier cream after drying. Absolutely no intercourse until the area is completely healed and you have started estrogen therapy.

SMS INFORMATION

Presumptive diagnosis: Vaginal atrophy with minor tear. Numbing gel applied. Strictly no intercourse. See GP next week for estrogen cream.

SMS EDUCATION

